    qDe: "Man bestimme alle Tripel (a, b, c) positiver ganzer Zahlen, für die die Bedingungen\n\nggT(a, 20) = b,   ggT(b, 15) = c   und   ggT(a, c) = 5\n\ngelten.",
    q: "Determine all triples (a, b, c) of positive integers for which the conditions\n\ngcd(a, 20) = b,   gcd(b, 15) = c   and   gcd(a, c) = 5\n\nhold.",
    techniques: ["gcd", "case analysis"],
    difficulty: 1,
    answer: "c = 5 always; b = 5 if a is an odd multiple of 5, b = 10 if a ≡ 10 (mod 20), b = 20 if 20 ∣ a. So (a, b, c) = (10t − 5, 5, 5), (20t − 10, 10, 5) or (20t, 20, 5) with t a positive integer.",
    hints: ["Which values can b take at all, given that b = gcd(a, 20)? And which values can c take?", "c divides 15 and 5 divides c, so c is 5 or 15. Can c be 15 when b divides 20?", "Once c = 5, you get 5 ∣ a. Now split according to how many factors 2 the number a has."],
    rubric: [["Shows c = 5 (c ∣ 15, 5 ∣ c, and c = 15 is impossible because b ∣ 20)", 2], ["Deduces 5 ∣ a and b ∈ {5, 10, 20}", 2], ["Correct case split by the power of 2 in a, giving all three families", 3], ["Checks that every listed triple really satisfies all three conditions", 1]],
    solution: "From gcd(a, c) = 5 we get 5 ∣ c, and from gcd(b, 15) = c we get c ∣ 15. Hence c ∈ {5, 15}. If c = 15, then 15 ∣ b; but b = gcd(a, 20) divides 20, which is not divisible by 3. So c = 5.\n\nNow gcd(a, 5) = 5 means 5 ∣ a. Then b = gcd(a, 20) is a multiple of 5 that divides 20, so b ∈ {5, 10, 20}, and in each case gcd(b, 15) = 5 = c holds automatically. Which value b takes depends only on the power of 2 in a:\n\n• a odd, i.e. a = 10t − 5: gcd(a, 20) = 5, so b = 5.\n\n• a ≡ 2 (mod 4), i.e. a = 20t − 10: gcd(a, 20) = 10, so b = 10.\n\n• 4 ∣ a, i.e. a = 20t: gcd(a, 20) = 20, so b = 20.\n\nConversely, each such triple works: gcd(a, 20) = b by the case distinction, gcd(b, 15) = 5 = c, and gcd(a, 5) = 5 since 5 ∣ a.\n\nAnswer: (a, b, c) = (10t − 5, 5, 5), (20t − 10, 10, 5) or (20t, 20, 5), where t is any positive integer.",
    pitfalls: ["Listing a few numerical examples instead of describing all (infinitely many) triples.", "Forgetting to rule out c = 15 (and c = 1, c = 3) explicitly."],
