    title: "One of three is at most 1",
    qDe: "Es seien x, y und z positive reelle Zahlen mit x + y + z = 3.\n\nMan beweise, dass mindestens eine der drei Zahlen\n\nx(x + y − z),   y(y + z − x)   oder   z(z + x − y)\n\nkleiner oder gleich 1 ist.",
    q: "Let x, y and z be positive real numbers with x + y + z = 3.\n\nProve that at least one of the three numbers\n\nx(x + y − z),   y(y + z − x)   or   z(z + x − y)\n\nis less than or equal to 1.",
    techniques: ["contradiction", "HM-AM"],
    difficulty: 2,
    answer: null,
    hints: ["Assume the opposite: all three numbers are greater than 1.", "Divide each of these inequalities by the positive variable in front, e.g. x + y − z > 1/x. What happens when you add all three?", "Adding gives 3 > 1/x + 1/y + 1/z. Compare with (x + y + z)(1/x + 1/y + 1/z) ≥ 9."],
    rubric: [["Sets up a proof by contradiction and rewrites as x + y − z > 1/x etc.", 2], ["Adds the three inequalities to obtain 3 > 1/x + 1/y + 1/z", 3], ["Contradiction via HM-AM or Cauchy-Schwarz using x + y + z = 3", 3]],
    solution: "Suppose, for contradiction, that all three numbers are greater than 1. Since x, y, z > 0 we may divide:\n\nx + y − z > 1/x,   y + z − x > 1/y,   z + x − y > 1/z.\n\nThe left-hand sides add up to x + y + z = 3, so adding the three inequalities gives\n\n3 > 1/x + 1/y + 1/z.\n\nOn the other hand, by the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality (equivalently HM ≤ AM), (x + y + z)(1/x + 1/y + 1/z) ≥ 9, so 1/x + 1/y + 1/z ≥ 9/3 = 3. This contradicts the previous line. Hence at least one of the three numbers is at most 1.\n\nRemark: the statement is sharp — for x = y = z = 1 all three numbers equal 1.",
    pitfalls: ["Assuming the brackets x + y − z etc. are positive (they need not be; the division argument does not need it).", "Trying to show that all three numbers are ≤ 1, which is false (x = y = 1.4, z = 0.2 gives x(x + y − z) = 3.64)."],
