    title: "Isosceles triangle, circle BIU",
    qDe: "Es sei ABC ein gleichschenkeliges Dreieck mit AC = BC und ∠ACB < 60°. Wir bezeichnen den Inkreismittelpunkt und den Umkreismittelpunkt mit I bzw. U. Der Umkreis des Dreiecks BIU schneidet den Schenkel BC ein zweites Mal im Punkt D.\n\n(a) Man beweise, dass die Geraden AC und DI parallel sind.\n\n(b) Man beweise, dass die Geraden UD und IB aufeinander normal stehen.",
    q: "Let ABC be an isosceles triangle with AC = BC and ∠ACB < 60°. Denote the incentre by I and the circumcentre by U. The circumcircle of triangle BIU meets the leg BC a second time at the point D.\n\n(a) Prove that the lines AC and DI are parallel.\n\n(b) Prove that the lines UD and IB are perpendicular.",
    techniques: ["angle chasing", "cyclic quadrilateral", "inscribed angle theorem"],
    difficulty: 3,
    answer: null,
    hints: ["Write α = ∠CAB = ∠ABC and γ = ∠ACB. Both I and U lie on the symmetry axis through C. In which order? (This is where γ < 60° is used.)", "For (a): compute ∠BUI from the central angle ∠BUC = 2α, then transfer it to D using the circle through B, I, U, D.", "For (b): compute ∠UDB from the same circle (∠UDB = ∠BIG with G the midpoint of AB) and look at the triangle formed by BD, BI and DU."],
    rubric: [["Locates I and U on the symmetry axis with U between C and I (uses γ < 60°)", 1], ["(a) ∠BUI = γ via the central angle ∠BUC = 2α", 2], ["(a) ∠BDI = ∠BUI, hence DI ∥ AC", 2], ["(b) ∠UDB = 90° − α/2 from the cyclic quadrilateral", 2], ["(b) Angle sum in the triangle formed by BD, BI, DU gives the right angle", 1]],
    solution: "Let α = ∠CAB = ∠ABC, γ = ∠ACB and let G be the midpoint of AB. Since γ < 60°, we have α = 90° − γ/2 > 60° > γ, and α < 90°.\n\nPosition of I and U. Both lie on the axis of symmetry CG. As a point X moves along CG from C to G, the angle ∠XBC grows from 0 to α. Triangle UBC is isosceles with ∠UCB = γ/2, so ∠UBC = γ/2, while ∠IBC = α/2 > γ/2. Hence the order on the axis is C, U, I, G (U strictly between C and I).\n\n(a) Since α < 90°, the central angle over BC is ∠BUC = 2α. Because U lies between C and I, ∠BUI = 180° − ∠BUC = 180° − 2α = γ. The points U and D lie on the same side of the line BI (U lies on segment CI, D on segment BC, and C is on that side). So, in the circle through B, I, U, D, the inscribed angles over BI are equal: ∠BDI = ∠BUI = γ = ∠BCA. As D lies on BC, these are corresponding angles, so DI ∥ CA.\n\n(b) The line BU separates C from I (U is an interior point of segment CI), and D lies on C's side; so D and I are on opposite sides of BU, and in the cyclic quadrilateral BDUI we get ∠UDB = 180° − ∠UIB. Since I lies between U and G, ∠UIB = 180° − ∠BIG, and in the right triangle BGI we have ∠BIG = 90° − ∠IBG = 90° − α/2. Hence ∠UDB = 90° − α/2.\n\nLet F be the intersection of the lines DU and BI. In triangle BDF, ∠DBF = ∠CBI = α/2 and ∠BDF = 90° − α/2, so ∠BFD = 180° − α/2 − (90° − α/2) = 90°. Thus UD ⊥ IB.",
    pitfalls: ["Using inscribed angles without checking on which side of the chord the points lie (the order C, U, I on the axis matters).", "Not using the hypothesis ∠ACB < 60° at all — it is exactly what fixes the order of U and I."],
