    q: "Determine all positive integers k and n satisfying the equation\n\nk² − 2016 = 3ⁿ.",
    techniques: ["modular arithmetic", "factorisation"],
    difficulty: 2,
    answer: "(k, n) = (45, 2)",
    hints: ["Factor 2016 = 2⁵ · 3² · 7 and look at the equation modulo 3.", "Show 3 ∣ k, hence 9 ∣ k², hence n ≥ 2; then divide the whole equation by 9.", "After dividing you get m² − 224 = 3ⁿ⁻² with k = 3m. Look at this modulo 3."],
    rubric: [["Shows 3 ∣ k and n ≥ 2", 2], ["Reduces to m² − 224 = 3ⁿ⁻² and proves n = 2 by working modulo 3", 4], ["Finds k = 45 and checks the solution", 2]],
    solution: "Since 3 ∣ 2016 and 3 ∣ 3ⁿ, we get 3 ∣ k², hence 3 ∣ k and 9 ∣ k². As 2016 = 9 · 224, it follows that 9 ∣ 3ⁿ, i.e. n ≥ 2. Write k = 3m and divide by 9:\n\nm² − 224 = 3ⁿ⁻².\n\nNow 224 ≡ 2 (mod 3) and m² ≡ 0 or 1 (mod 3), so m² − 224 ≡ 1 or 2 (mod 3); it is not divisible by 3. Hence 3ⁿ⁻² is not divisible by 3, which forces n = 2. Then m² = 225, m = 15 and k = 45.\n\nCheck: 45² − 2016 = 2025 − 2016 = 9 = 3². So the only solution is (k, n) = (45, 2).\n\n(Alternative: modulo 4 one gets k² ≡ (−1)ⁿ, so n = 2t is even and (k − 3ᵗ)(k + 3ᵗ) = 2016; checking the factor pairs of 2016 into two even factors again gives only k = 45, t = 1.)",
    pitfalls: ["Finding (45, 2) by trial without proving there are no further solutions — this earns very little.", "In the factor-pair approach: forgetting that both factors must be even, or overlooking a pair."],
