    q: "Let a, b, c and d be real numbers with a² + b² + c² + d² = 4.\n\nProve that the inequality\n\n(a + 2)(b + 2) ≥ cd\n\nholds, and give four numbers a, b, c and d for which equality holds.",
    techniques: ["SOS", "substitution", "equality case"],
    difficulty: 2,
    answer: "Equality e.g. for a = b = −1, c = d = 1 (in general exactly when a + b = −2 and c = d, together with a² + b² + c² + d² = 4).",
    hints: ["Expand the left side. Where can you use a² + b² + c² + d² = 4 to replace the constant 4?", "cd ≤ (c² + d²)/2, and c² + d² = 4 − a² − b².", "Everything should collapse to a perfect square in a + b. When is it zero?"],
    rubric: [["Uses the constraint to replace the constant and brings everything to one side", 2], ["Writes the difference as (a + b + 2)² + (c − d)² (or an equivalent square/AM-GM argument)", 4], ["Gives a valid equality case and checks the constraint", 2]],
    solution: "Expanding, we must show ab + 2a + 2b + 4 − cd ≥ 0. Multiply by 2 and write 2 · 4 = 4 + (a² + b² + c² + d²), using the condition once:\n\n2ab + 4a + 4b + 8 − 2cd = 2ab + 4a + 4b + 4 + a² + b² + c² + d² − 2cd.\n\nRearranging,\n\n= (a² + 2ab + b² + 4a + 4b + 4) + (c² − 2cd + d²) = (a + b + 2)² + (c − d)² ≥ 0.\n\nThis proves the inequality. Equality holds exactly when a + b = −2 and c = d (with the constraint). For example a = b = −1, c = d = 1: then a² + b² + c² + d² = 4 and (a + 2)(b + 2) = 1 · 1 = 1 = cd.",
