    title: "n = a² + b² with divisors",
    qDe: "Man bestimme alle natürlichen Zahlen n ≥ 2, für die\n\nn = a² + b²\n\ngilt, wobei a der kleinste von 1 verschiedene Teiler von n und b ein beliebiger Teiler von n ist.",
    q: "Determine all natural numbers n ≥ 2 for which\n\nn = a² + b²\n\nholds, where a is the smallest divisor of n different from 1 and b is any divisor of n.",
    techniques: ["parity", "divisibility", "case analysis"],
    difficulty: 1,
    answer: "n = 8 and n = 20",
    hints: ["What can a be? Start with the parity of n.", "If n is odd, all its divisors are odd. What is the parity of a² + b² then?", "With a = 2 you get n = b² + 4 and b ∣ n. What must b divide?"],
    rubric: [["Shows n must be even, hence a = 2", 3], ["Derives b ∣ 4 from b ∣ n = b² + 4", 3], ["Checks the three cases and states n = 8, 20", 2]],
    solution: "Let a be the smallest divisor of n greater than 1.\n\nn is even: if n were odd, then all its divisors, in particular a and b, would be odd, and a² + b² would be even — a contradiction. Hence 2 ∣ n and a = 2, so n = b² + 4.\n\nSince b ∣ n and b ∣ b², we get b ∣ n − b² = 4, so b ∈ {1, 2, 4}.\n\n• b = 1: n = 5 is odd — impossible (its smallest divisor > 1 is 5, not 2).\n\n• b = 2: n = 8. Its smallest divisor > 1 is 2, and 2 ∣ 8. Valid.\n\n• b = 4: n = 20. Its smallest divisor > 1 is 2, and 4 ∣ 20. Valid.\n\nAnswer: n = 8 and n = 20.",
    pitfalls: ["Missing the case b = 4 (b may be larger than a).", "Not checking that the candidates really have 2 as their smallest divisor and b as a divisor."],
