    q: "The convex pentagon ABCDE has a circumcircle, and AB = BD. Let P be the intersection of the diagonals AC and BE. The lines BC and DE meet at the point Q.\n\nProve that the line PQ is parallel to the diagonal AD.",
    techniques: ["cyclic quadrilateral", "inscribed angle theorem", "angle chasing"],
    difficulty: 3,
    answer: null,
    hints: ["Translate AB = BD into equal inscribed angles: which angles stand on the chords AB and BD?", "Show ∠BED = ∠ACB. What does this say about the four points P, C, Q, E?", "In the circle through P, C, Q, E compare ∠EQP with ∠ECA, and then ∠ECA with ∠EDA in the big circle."],
    rubric: [["Uses AB = BD to get ∠BED = ∠BAD = ∠ADB = ∠ACB", 3], ["Proves that P, C, Q, E are concyclic", 2], ["Transfers angles: ∠EQP = ∠ECA = ∠EDA", 2], ["Concludes PQ ∥ AD", 1]],
    solution: "Position of Q. Lines BC and ED meet beyond C and D (on the far side from A) iff ∠BCD + ∠CDE > 180°. By inscribed angles, ∠BCD = 180° − ∠BAD and ∠CDE = 180° − ∠CBE, so this means ∠BAD + ∠CBE < 180°, i.e. arc BCD + arc CDE < 360° (arcs not containing the vertex). Since AB = BD, arc AB = arc BCD = arc BC + arc CD, and the condition becomes arc CD < arc AB + arc EA, which is true. So C lies between B and Q, and D between E and Q.\n\nEqual angles. In the isosceles triangle ABD (AB = BD) we have ∠BAD = ∠ADB. By the inscribed angle theorem, ∠BED = ∠BAD (both on chord BD, E and A on the same arc) and ∠ACB = ∠ADB (both on chord AB). Hence ∠BED = ∠ACB.\n\nCyclic quadrilateral. Since P lies on segment EB and Q on ray ED, ∠PEQ = ∠BED. Since P lies on segment CA and C lies between B and Q, ∠QCP = 180° − ∠BCA. So ∠QCP + ∠PEQ = 180°, and the quadrilateral PCQE is cyclic.\n\nParallel lines. In this circle, ∠EQP = ∠ECP (both on chord EP). Since P lies on CA, ∠ECP = ∠ECA, and in the circumcircle of the pentagon ∠ECA = ∠EDA (both on chord EA). Hence ∠EQP = ∠EDA. As D lies on the ray EQ, the lines QP and DA make equal corresponding angles with the line EQ, so PQ ∥ AD.",
