    q: "Determine all natural numbers n that are smaller than 128⁹⁷ and have exactly 2019 divisors.",
    techniques: ["divisor counting", "size bounds"],
    difficulty: 2,
    answer: "n = 2⁶⁷² · 3², 2⁶⁷² · 5², 2⁶⁷² · 7², 2⁶⁷² · 11²",
    hints: ["Use the divisor-count formula d(n) = (e₁ + 1)(e₂ + 1)⋯ and factor 2019.", "2019 = 3 · 673 (both prime). Which exponent patterns are possible? Write 128⁹⁷ as a power of 2.", "The huge exponent 672 must sit on the prime 2. How large can the second prime be?"],
    rubric: [["Divisor-count formula and 2019 = 3 · 673 give the shapes p²⁰¹⁸ and p⁶⁷² q²", 3], ["Rewrites 128⁹⁷ = 2⁶⁷⁹ and excludes p²⁰¹⁸ and p ≥ 3", 3], ["Finds q ∈ {3, 5, 7, 11}", 2]],
    solution: "If n = p₁^e₁ ⋯ p_k^e_k, then d(n) = (e₁ + 1)⋯(e_k + 1). Since 2019 = 3 · 673 with 3 and 673 prime, either n = p²⁰¹⁸ for a prime p, or n = p⁶⁷² q² with distinct primes p and q.\n\nThe bound is 128⁹⁷ = (2⁷)⁹⁷ = 2⁶⁷⁹.\n\n• n = p²⁰¹⁸ ≥ 2²⁰¹⁸ > 2⁶⁷⁹: impossible.\n\n• n = p⁶⁷² q² with p ≥ 3: n > 3⁶⁷² = 9³³⁶ > 8³³⁶ = 2¹⁰⁰⁸ > 2⁶⁷⁹: impossible.\n\n• So p = 2 and n = 2⁶⁷² q² < 2⁶⁷⁹ iff q² < 2⁷ = 128 iff q ≤ 11. With q an odd prime: q ∈ {3, 5, 7, 11}.\n\nHence there are exactly four such numbers: 2⁶⁷² · 9, 2⁶⁷² · 25, 2⁶⁷² · 49 and 2⁶⁷² · 121.",
    pitfalls: ["Misreading the bound: it is 128 to the power 97, i.e. 2⁶⁷⁹.", "Allowing q = 2 (that would be 2⁶⁷⁴ with only 675 divisors)."],
