    area: "G",
    title: "Rhombus and a circle around A",
    qDe: "Sei ABCD eine Raute mit ∠BAD < 90°. Der Kreis durch D mit Mittelpunkt A schneide die Gerade CD ein zweites Mal im Punkt E. Der Schnittpunkt der Geraden BE und AC sei S.\n\nMan beweise, dass die Punkte A, S, D und E auf einem Kreis liegen.",
    q: "Let ABCD be a rhombus with ∠BAD < 90°. The circle through D with centre A meets the line CD a second time at the point E. Let S be the intersection of the lines BE and AC.\n\nProve that the points A, S, D and E lie on a circle.",
    techniques: ["inscribed angle theorem", "angle chasing", "cyclic quadrilateral"],
    difficulty: 2,
    answer: null,
    hints: ["Which other vertex of the rhombus lies on the circle with centre A through D?", "AC is the perpendicular bisector of BD, so triangle SBD is isosceles.", "Compare the exterior angle ∠DSE of triangle SBD with the central angle ∠DAE."],
    rubric: [["B lies on the circle with centre A (AB = AD), so ∠DBE = ½ ∠DAE", 3], ["SB = SD because AC is the perpendicular bisector of BD", 2], ["∠DSE = 2∠DBE = ∠DAE (exterior angle)", 2], ["Concludes that A, S, D, E are concyclic", 1]],
    solution: "Let α = ∠BAD < 90°. Then ∠ADC = 180° − α is obtuse, so the foot of the perpendicular from A to the line CD lies beyond D, and E (the reflection of D in that foot) lies on the extension of CD beyond D. Hence ∠ADE = 180° − ∠ADC = α, and in the isosceles triangle ADE (AD = AE) also ∠AED = α, so ∠DAE = 180° − 2α.\n\nSince AB = AD, the point B also lies on the circle with centre A through D and E. The inscribed angle ∠DBE in this circle is half the central angle over the chord DE: ∠DBE = ½(180° − 2α) = 90° − α (it is acute, so B lies on the major arc and the central angle is ∠DAE).\n\nThe diagonal AC of the rhombus is the perpendicular bisector of BD, so SB = SD and ∠SDB = ∠SBD = ∠EBD = 90° − α (S lies between B and E). The angle ∠DSE is the exterior angle at S of triangle BSD, hence\n\n∠DSE = ∠SBD + ∠SDB = 180° − 2α = ∠DAE.\n\nSo S and A see the segment DE under the same angle, from the same side of the line DE; by the converse of the inscribed angle theorem, A, S, D, E lie on a circle.",
    pitfalls: ["Not noticing that B lies on the circle — then ∠DBE is hard to get.", "Placing E between C and D (it lies on the extension of CD beyond D because ∠ADC is obtuse)."],
