    qDe: "Man bestimme alle natürlichen Zahlen n ≥ 2, für die es zwei Anordnungen (a₁, a₂, …, aₙ) und (b₁, b₂, …, bₙ) der Zahlen 1, 2, …, n gibt, sodass (a₁ + b₁, a₂ + b₂, …, aₙ + bₙ) aufeinander folgende natürliche Zahlen sind.",
    q: "Determine all natural numbers n ≥ 2 for which there exist two arrangements (a₁, a₂, …, aₙ) and (b₁, b₂, …, bₙ) of the numbers 1, 2, …, n such that (a₁ + b₁, a₂ + b₂, …, aₙ + bₙ) are consecutive natural numbers.",
    techniques: ["double counting", "construction", "parity"],
    difficulty: 2,
    answer: "Exactly the odd n (n ≥ 3).",
    hints: ["Add up all n sums in two different ways.", "If the sums are N, N + 1, …, N + n − 1, compute N. When is it an integer?", "For n = 2k + 1, try aᵢ = i and bᵢ = i + k for i ≤ k + 1, bᵢ = i − k − 1 for i ≥ k + 2."],
    rubric: [["Sum argument: n(n + 1) = nN + n(n − 1)/2", 2], ["Concludes that n must be odd", 2], ["Explicit construction for every odd n", 3], ["Verification that the construction works", 1]],
    solution: "Necessity. Since both arrangements are permutations of 1, …, n, the sum of all aᵢ + bᵢ is 2(1 + 2 + … + n) = n(n + 1). If the sums are the consecutive numbers N, N + 1, …, N + n − 1 (in some order — reordering the indices simultaneously does not matter), their sum is nN + n(n − 1)/2. Hence n(n + 1) = nN + n(n − 1)/2, i.e. N = (n + 3)/2. As N is an integer, n must be odd.\n\nSufficiency. Let n = 2k + 1 with k ≥ 1. Put aᵢ = i for all i, and\n\nbᵢ = i + k for 1 ≤ i ≤ k + 1,   bᵢ = i − k − 1 for k + 2 ≤ i ≤ 2k + 1.\n\nThe values bᵢ run through k + 1, …, 2k + 1 and then 1, …, k, so (bᵢ) is an arrangement of 1, …, n. The sums are aᵢ + bᵢ = 2i + k for i ≤ k + 1, giving k + 2, k + 4, …, 3k + 2, and 2i − k − 1 for i ≥ k + 2, giving k + 3, k + 5, …, 3k + 1. Together these are exactly the 2k + 1 consecutive numbers k + 2, k + 3, …, 3k + 2. (Example n = 3: b = (2, 3, 1), sums 3, 5, 4.)\n\nAnswer: exactly the odd numbers n ≥ 3.",
    pitfalls: ["Proving only that n must be odd, or giving the construction only for small n.", "Forgetting that n = 1 is excluded by n ≥ 2 (the answer is all odd n ≥ 3)."],
