    rubric: [["Substitution x = da, y = db with gcd(a, b) = 1, giving d²ab = a + b + d", 2], ["Case d = 1: (a − 1)(b − 1) = 2", 2], ["Case d ≥ 2: a bound forces d = 2 and a = b = 1", 3], ["All three solutions, checked", 1]],
    solution: "Write x = da and y = db with gcd(a, b) = 1. Dividing the equation d³ab = da + db + d² by d gives\n\nd²ab = a + b + d.\n\nSince (a − 1)(b − 1) ≥ 0, we have a + b ≤ ab + 1, so d²ab ≤ ab + 1 + d, i.e. (d² − 1)ab ≤ d + 1, i.e. (d − 1)ab ≤ 1 (divide by d + 1).\n\n• d = 1: ab = a + b + 1, i.e. (a − 1)(b − 1) = 2, so {a, b} = {2, 3}. These are coprime, giving (x, y) = (2, 3) and (3, 2).\n\n• d ≥ 2: then (d − 1)ab ≤ 1 forces d = 2 and a = b = 1, i.e. x = y = 2. Check: d = gcd(2, 2) = 2 and 2 · 2 · 2 = 8 = 2 + 2 + 4.\n\nAnswer: (x, y) = (2, 2), (2, 3), (3, 2).",
    pitfalls: ["Forgetting to check the gcd condition for candidates (e.g. (1, 5) satisfies 2xy = x + y + 4 but has gcd 1).", "Missing the symmetric pair (3, 2)."],
