    title: "Airy sets and factorials",
    qDe: "Sei n eine positive ganze Zahl. Eine Menge positiver ganzer Zahlen heiße luftig, wenn sie keine zwei benachbarten Zahlen enthält. Für jede luftige Teilmenge der Menge {1, 2, …, n} wird das Produkt ihrer Elemente berechnet, dann werden die Quadrate aller dieser Produkte addiert, wobei der leeren Teilmenge das Produkt 1 zugewiesen wird.\n\nMan bestimme diese Summe sₙ.\n\nBeispiel: n = 3\n\nDie luftigen Teilmengen von {1, 2, 3} sind {}, {1}, {2}, {3} und {1, 3}. Für die Produkte erhalten wir:\n{} | {1} | {2} | {3} | {1, 3}\n1 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 3\nDie Summe der Quadrate ergibt also s₃ = 1² + 1² + 2² + 3² + 3² = 24.",
    q: "Let n be a positive integer. A set of positive integers is called airy if it contains no two neighbouring (consecutive) numbers. For every airy subset of the set {1, 2, …, n} the product of its elements is computed; then the squares of all these products are added, where the empty subset is assigned the product 1.\n\nDetermine this sum sₙ.\n\nExample: n = 3\n\nThe airy subsets of {1, 2, 3} are {}, {1}, {2}, {3} and {1, 3}. For the products we obtain:\n{} | {1} | {2} | {3} | {1, 3}\n1 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 3\nSo the sum of the squares is s₃ = 1² + 1² + 2² + 3² + 3² = 24.",
    techniques: ["recursion", "induction"],
    difficulty: 2,
    answer: "sₙ = (n + 1)!",
    hints: ["Compute s₁, s₂, s₃, s₄ and compare with factorials.", "Split the airy subsets of {1, …, n} according to whether they contain n. If they do, they cannot contain n − 1.", "This gives sₙ = sₙ₋₁ + n² · sₙ₋₂. Prove sₙ = (n + 1)! by induction with two base cases."],
    rubric: [["Correct recursion sₙ = sₙ₋₁ + n² sₙ₋₂ with justification", 4], ["Initial values and the conjecture (n + 1)!", 1], ["Induction proof", 3]],
    solution: "Small values: s₁ = 1² + 1² = 2, s₂ = 1 + 1 + 4 = 6, s₃ = 24, s₄ = 120. This suggests sₙ = (n + 1)!. It is convenient to set s₀ = 1 (only the empty set).\n\nRecursion. Let n ≥ 2 and split the airy subsets T of {1, …, n} into two kinds.\n\n• n ∉ T: these are exactly the airy subsets of {1, …, n − 1}; they contribute sₙ₋₁.\n\n• n ∈ T: then n − 1 ∉ T, and T = T′ ∪ {n} where T′ is an arbitrary airy subset of {1, …, n − 2}. The product of T is n times the product of T′, so its square is n² times the square for T′. These sets contribute n² · sₙ₋₂.\n\nHence sₙ = sₙ₋₁ + n² · sₙ₋₂ for n ≥ 2 (with s₀ = 1, s₁ = 2).\n\nInduction. sₙ = (n + 1)! holds for n = 0 and n = 1. If it holds for n − 2 and n − 1, then\n\nsₙ = n! + n² · (n − 1)! = n! + n · n! = (n + 1) · n! = (n + 1)!.\n\nTherefore sₙ = (n + 1)! for every positive integer n.",
    pitfalls: ["Using only one base case for a two-step recursion.", "Forgetting the empty set (product 1) when computing the starting values."],
